.TH OPEN 5
.SH NAME
lopen, lcreate, mkdir \- open, create or make a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tlopen tag[2] fid[4] flags[4]
.B size[4] Rlopen tag[2] qid[13] iounit[4]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Tlcreate tag[2] fid[4] name[s] flags[4] mode[4] gid[4]
.B size[4] Rlcreate tag[2] qid[13] iounit[4]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Tmkdir tag[2] dfid[4] name[s] mode[4] gid[4]
.B size[4] Rmkdir tag[2] qid[13]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tlopen
prepares
.I fid
for I/O, and asks the server to check permissions.
.I Flags
are Linux's open flags: the access mode in the low bits,
.BR O_CREAT ,
.B O_TRUNC
and the rest above them, exactly as
.IR open (2)
takes them.
A fid that is a directory is opened the same way, and its
.I flags
are checked against
.BR O_RDONLY .
.PP
.B Tlcreate
asks the server to create a file named
.I name
in the directory
.I fid
names, and requires write permission there.
On success
.I fid
itself becomes the new file, open under
.IR flags ,
so a caller that wants to keep a handle on the directory clones its
fid first.
See
.IR walk (5).
.I Gid
is carried because the message has it, and is not read back by
anything in the tree.
.PP
.B Tmkdir
is the same request for a directory, and differs in shape because
9P2000.L answers it with a
.I qid
rather than opening a fid:
.I dfid
stays the parent, and a client that wants the new directory walks to
it by name afterwards.
.PP
.I Iounit
is the largest read or write the server promises to do without
breaking it into more than one message, or zero for no promise.
A caller that ignores it and chunks by
.I msize
less
.B IOHDRSZ
still works; one that honours
.I iounit
instead never sends a request larger than the server actually
promised to answer whole.
See
.IR intro (5).
.PP
A fid already open for I/O is refused a further
.B Tlopen
or
.BR Tlcreate ,
answering
.BR EINVAL .
A server that serves a fixed, read-only tree refuses
.B Tlcreate
and
.B Tmkdir
outright, with
.B EPERM
where the tree's shape is not the client's to change,
and refuses
.B Tlopen
for anything but
.B O_RDONLY
with
.BR EROFS .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/chan.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/walk.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR walk (5),
.IR read (5),
.IR getattr (5),
.IR vectra9 (2)
